5:[["$","$L10","2.5",{"topic":{"id":"2.5","title":"Sources of Electronic Evidence","minutes":12,"summary":"Look beyond the visible document to devices, applications, networks, cloud services, and the people who control them.","sections":[{"title":"Endpoint and removable-media artifacts","body":["Computers can contain documents, browser history, application databases, registry hives, event logs, shortcut files, shellbags, print artifacts, and memory dumps. External drives, memory cards, and phones may contain additional copies or synchronization records. The presence of an artifact can support a timeline, but its meaning depends on the operating system and application version.","Record device make, model, serial number, storage identifiers, state, connected peripherals, and relevant user accounts. A phone may depend on a paired cloud account; a laptop may contain only placeholders for cloud-hosted files. Do not assume that the device in front of you is the only or original source of every displayed record."]},{"title":"Network and service-provider records","body":["Routers, firewalls, proxy servers, DNS logs, DHCP leases, authentication systems, and packet captures can connect a local device to network activity. DHCP is particularly useful when several devices share a public IP address. Provider records may include subscriber details, access logs, payment records, or content, each with different access conditions and retention periods.","Email headers can reveal a submission path, but received headers can be forged and internal paths may be obscured by relays. Payment records, merchant references, and account-control evidence can be more probative than an IP address alone. Ask for the provider’s interpretation of fields and time zones, and preserve the response in its original format."]},{"title":"Cloud, wearable, and human sources","body":["Cloud audit logs, object versions, administrative actions, snapshots, and account tokens can reconstruct events outside a seized device. Smart watches, vehicles, cameras, and IoT applications may hold location or activity traces. Their data can be synchronized or transformed, so identify whether the export came from the device, a phone, or a remote service.","Witness statements, business-process records, and consent or authorization documents explain technical traces. A bank employee may confirm that a particular reference represents a settlement, not merely a payment request. Build a source matrix with custodian, location, acquisition method, expected retention, and legal limitations. This avoids overlooking short-lived evidence."]}],"practice":{"title":"Create a source matrix","scenario":"A disputed purchase was made through a shared home computer and a mobile payment app.","steps":["List endpoint, local network, provider, bank, and human sources.","Identify who controls each source.","Mark records likely to expire first.","Connect each source to a specific question, such as device-to-account association."],"debrief":"A shared public IP is only one clue. Local leases, authentication events, and payment records may distinguish devices and account control."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bnss","bsa","it-act"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bnss"],"labId":"network","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain endpoint and removable-media artifacts and apply it to an investigation.","Explain network and service-provider records and apply it to an investigation.","Explain cloud, wearable, and human sources and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.5","prompt":"Why can DHCP logs be important in a shared-network investigation?","options":["They prove the identity of every user.","They may associate an internal address with a device at a particular time.","They decrypt all encrypted traffic.","They replace the need for lawful authority."],"answer":1,"explanation":"DHCP records can help connect address assignments to devices, subject to clock accuracy and reliable interpretation.","unit":2}}}]]
